                        ) (Table 4 and Fig. 7
                        ). In other words, decrease in the ternary interaction parameter guaranteed increase in the equilibrium swelling ratio of the system. To further clarify the behavior, the least equilibrium swelling ratio corresponded to the blend containing the highest amount of MBC, which was incompatible with water (Table 4). Increase in the PG content to 30 wt. % increased the equilibrium swelling ratio of the blend as a result of compatibility of PG with water. Although all MBC-PG blends containing more than 30 wt. % PG swelled more than MBC-PG6 because of the higher amount of PG, enhancement in PG content from 30 wt. % up to 45 wt. % deteriorated the swelling behavior of the blend as a consequence of the diminished film deformability. In other words, swelling as a complex phenomenon, which depends on both polymer-solvent affinity and polymer deformability (Vayer et al., 2017), decreased due to the addition of more glassy phase-separated agglomerates in the system. The reduced deformability of the blend film, as a competitive factor to the affinity of PG toward water, was verified with decrement in the 
                           k
                         parameter reported in Table 3. However, further increase in the PG content, despite reducing the deformability of the polymer blend, improved the swelling behavior owing to the transition of the phase behavior of the blend toward molecular miscibility, which facilitated the accessibility of water to the molecularly dispersed hydrophilic PG.
